2. Design & Analysis 
PSEUDOCODE: RUNNING-SUM MOVING AVERAGE 
FUNCTION MovingAverage(readings, k=7): 
    IF length(readings) < k: RETURN empty list 
    window_sum = SUM(readings[0 : k]) 
    result = [window_sum / k] 
    FOR i FROM k TO length(readings) - 1: 
        window_sum = window_sum + readings[i] - readings[i - k] 
        APPEND (window_sum / k) TO result 
    RETURN result 
 
• readings[7:14]: Returns elements at indices 7 through 13 (the second week of the year, days 8–14). 
• readings[-7:]: Returns the last 7 elements of the list (the final 7 days of the year). 
• readings[::7]: Steps through the entire list with stride 7, returning every 7th day (indices 0, 7, 14, ..., 364). 
• List Suitability & Operation Costs: Backed by a contiguous dynamic array of pointers: O(1) random index 
access, O(1) amortized append(), O(n) insert(i, x) and pop(i) due to element shifting, O(1) pop() at end, and O(n 
log n) sort() via Timsort. 
• Derivation of Additions (n days, k = 7): Naive approach sums k elements across (n - 6) windows = 6(n - 6) = 6n - 
36 additions (O(k*n)). Running-sum performs 6 initial additions plus 2 operations (1 add, 1 subtract) for (n - 7) 
steps = 2n - 8 operations (O(n)). For n = 365: 2,154 vs 722 operations (~3x fewer). 
3. Experimental Investigation & Edge Cases 
