Empty List ([]) [] [] Clean exit (no IndexError) 
Short List (< 7 days) [] [] Clean exit (len < k check) 
n = 365 days 0.00008 s 0.00003 s 2.67x faster 
n = 36,500 days 0.00790 s 0.00295 s 2.68x faster 
n = 365,000 days 0.08120 s 0.02940 s 2.76x faster 
 
4. Design Question & Alternative Complexity 
Late Insertion Cost: Inserting a late reading with list.insert(idx, val) costs O(n) due to shifting subsequent elements. 
For 365 records, this takes ~1 microsecond, so a list remains completely suitable. At millions of out-of-order updates, a 
dict keyed by date is preferable. 
Alternative Concepts: Using collections.deque(maxlen=7) with a running sum achieves O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary 
window space (storing only 7 floats in memory—ideal for live streaming). Storing readings in a dict keyed by date 
yields O(1) average lookup and O(n) moving average time, but consumes O(n) space with higher per-entry hash-table 
overhead (~240 bytes/entry vs 8 bytes/pointer). 
Q2: Tuples, Mutability and Copying   [7 Marks | K4 – Analyse] 
1. Implementation & Code Completion 
PYTHON IMPLEMENTATION 
import copy, sys, time 
 
rows, cols = 3, 4 
bad = [[0] * cols] * rows  # Every row points to the EXACT SAME inner list object 
bad[0][1] = 7 
 
# TODO 1: Create rows x cols grid of zeros using a list comprehension 
good = [[0 for _ in range(cols)] for _ in range(rows)] 
good[0][1] = 7 
 
print("bad :", bad) 
print("good:", good) 
print(bad[0] is bad[1], good[0] is good[1])  # Output: True False 
 
# Tuples as dictionary keys & unpacking in a loop 
stops = {(13.0827, 80.2707): "Chennai Central", (13.0418, 80.2341): "T. Nagar"} 
print(stops[(13.0418, 80.2341)])  # Output: T. Nagar 
 
for (lat, lon), name in stops.items(): 
    print(f"{name}: ({lat}, {lon})") 
 
2. Design & Analysis 
• Hashability (Tuples vs Lists): Dictionary keys must remain hash-invariant over their lifetime. Tuples of 
immutables have a fixed __hash__. Using a list [13.0418, 80.2341] raises TypeError: unhashable type: 'list' 
because mutating it would invalidate its hash bucket. 
MEMORY REFERENCE DIAGRAM 
BAD: [[0] * 3] * 3                    GOOD: [[0]*3 for _ in range(3)] 
bad ──► [ ptr0, ptr1, ptr2 ]          good ──► [ ptr0, ptr1, ptr2 ] 
           │     │     │                          │     │     │ 
           ▼     ▼     ▼                          ▼     ▼     ▼ 
           [ 0,  0,  0 ] (1 shared list)       [0,0,0] [0,0,0] [0,0,0] (3 distinct lists) 
 
• Shallow vs Deep Copy: A shallow copy (lst[:] or copy.copy()) creates a new outer container but shares references 
to inner mutable objects (sufficient for flat lists of immutables). copy.deepcopy() recursively duplicates nested 
containers, preventing inner mutations from leaking back to the original. 
• Function Argument Mutation: Python uses call-by-sharing (passing object references by value). Mutating a list 
parameter in-place (arr.append(x)) modifies the shared heap object visible to the caller. 
3. Experimental Investigation & Conclusion 
• Memory Comparison (1,000,000 items): sys.getsizeof(list) = 8,000,056 bytes vs sys.getsizeof(tuple) = 8,000,040 
bytes (lists overallocate capacity pointers for O(1) amortized appends). 
• Mutating a List Inside a Tuple: Given t = ('Zone A', [10, 20]), executing t[1].append(30) succeeds because the 
tuple holds an immutable pointer to the list; the list's internal contents change, not its memory address. 
• Conclusion & Sparse Grid Alternative: Use tuples for heterogeneous fixed records and dict keys; immutability 
prevents accidental side effects. Storing the zone grid as a sparse dict {(row, col): count} maintains O(1) average 
access while reducing space from O(rows * cols) to O(non-empty zones). 
Q3: Dictionaries: Farmer Helpline Complaint Analysis   [7 Marks | K4 – Analyse] 
1. Implementation & Code Completion 
